[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Node types come in four families: the definition grid, coverage, representation and work

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-NODE-FAMILIES
TITLE: Node types come in four families: the definition grid, coverage, representation and work
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A durable claim is found that fits none of the four families, or a family proves to have no node after real use.
STATEMENT: >>>
The canon's node types are organised in four families, and only the first is
a grid.

DEFINITION -- the claims the canon asserts. Two axes: normative or descriptive
(can it be violated, or only be wrong?), and universal or particular (every
case, or one case?).

                  universal                    particular
  normative       REQUIREMENT                  DECISION
                  what must always hold        what was chosen here
  descriptive     MECHANISM                    EVIDENCE
                  how it always behaves        what was observed once

COVERAGE -- USE_CASE. A path the specification must enable. It is neither
violated nor wrong; it is covered or uncovered, by the requirements, decisions
and mechanisms it points at with Covered_By.

REPRESENTATION -- NARRATIVE today; code samples, diagrams and other maintained
projections later. A representation presents parts of the canon to a reader
and depends on their wording: it Cites what it presents, may fingerprint each
citation, and those edges go dirty when a cited node changes. A representation
is never itself a claim, and nothing in the definition grid may depend on one.

WORK -- something to do. Not a claim; it Crosses the mechanisms it builds on,
Produces the evidence it leaves behind, and dies with its plan.

Two placements change from DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID, which this decision supersedes.
A DECISION sits inside the grid as normative and particular: once accepted it
is a choice the specification must follow, so it can be violated like a
requirement; while open it is not yet in force, which keeps
DEC-OPEN-IS-THE-QUESTION intact. A USE CASE leaves the grid for its own family,
because its failure state -- uncovered -- is neither of the grid's two. The
grid thereby stops predicting a missing type and becomes a classification of
four; that loss of self-justification is accepted and stated here so nobody
rediscovers it.

Each family fails differently, and that is what its validators are for:
requirement satisfied or violated; decision followed or violated; mechanism
correct or wrong; evidence valid, wrong or outdated; use case covered or
uncovered; a representation's edge clean or dirty; work complete or incomplete.
These name validator families, not fields: only the last two can be computed
today, and a field nothing can set is a field nobody trusts. Each type keeps
its own state field and its own name (DEPTH, STATUS); there is no shared STATUS.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Offered by the operator from a fresh-lens session on 2026-08-30 and reconciled
against the canon. The machinery already treated a decision as a norm: 65
Governed_By edges point at decisions exactly as they would at requirements, and
the grid ruling itself asked Governed_By to admit a requirement, so "outside the
grid" survived only in prose. A use case's failure was already described as "the
design does not survive it", which is uncovered, not violated. The grid's own
RETIRES_ON -- a durable claim that fits no cell -- fired on that argument, so
this is the grid retiring by its own terms.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's delegation of
that day ("I'll trust you to take first pass at making decisions"); the operator
overrules by superseding. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag
for the human rungs. The concrete type set is unchanged from the grid, so the
retype of existing nodes is identical under both placements; five nodes cite
the grid by Governed_By and none had signed it, so nothing went dirty. Whether
code samples, diagrams and other representation types join the family is
DEC-INLINE-CODE-TYPE's, open in the whiteboard-view plan. PLAN as a type is
still DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN's.

Evidence is free-form, and structure is optional: a probe leaves code, research leaves a report or an assessment, a reproduction is a series of steps, a log is a log. A model reads it as context. SOURCE names where it came from when that is outside this repository. (The operator's note of 2026-08-30.)
<<<
